WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.520
I sat last night with Olive, and we were reading

2
00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:09.430
the latest in the "She persisted," series.

3
00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:11.610
I am such a fan of these books.

4
00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.360
This one is science.

5
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:14.193
Yes.

6
00:00:14.193 --> 00:00:17.430 line:15%
I want young readers to think, oh my gosh,

7
00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:22.430 line:15%
I can go off and discover amazing things is about animals

8
00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:25.420
I might not even know yet, like Jane Goodall,

9
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.980
or I can have amazing scientific discoveries

10
00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:29.750
like Rosalind Franklin.

11
00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:32.730
I've found so many of the kids that read early versions

12
00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:34.992
of "She Persisted in Science,"

13
00:00:34.992 --> 00:00:38.220
were actually particularly drawn to Flossie Wong-Staal

14
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.700
and to Rosalind Franklin, because their work

15
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.670
has also helped us understand COVID.

16
00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:42.503
Yeah.
And has helped in the

17
00:00:42.503 --> 00:00:44.050
development of the COVID-19 vaccines.

18
00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:47.650
And so I think it also is just great for kids to understand,

19
00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:50.430
oh my gosh, these incredible things that women did

20
00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:53.550
decades ago are now mattering to my life,

21
00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.330
are now helping to save people's lives today.

22
00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:59.284
And I appreciated reading-
(audience applauding)

23
00:00:59.284 --> 00:01:03.320
A children's book which very much for any age,

24
00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:06.760
and they're just so illuminating

25
00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.770
about what women, extraordinary women, have done.

26
00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.480
It's so celebratory, it's so inspirational.

27
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.735
So when I was reading the book with my children,

28
00:01:16.735 --> 00:01:20.073
in an earlier version, my son Aidan, who's five,

29
00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:25.130
was fascinated with Gladys West who helped invent GPS.

30
00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:26.617
Yes!
And he was like,

31
00:01:26.617 --> 00:01:29.470
"What? You used to not know where you were going?"

32
00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:32.900
And I was like, "Yes, way back when I was your age,

33
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.140
we got lost sometimes."
(audience laughing)

34
00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.871
He couldn't believe that there was a...

35
00:01:37.871 --> 00:01:40.650
He was like, "You didn't have a phone with a map on it?"

36
00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:44.568
And I was like, "No, I didn't even have a phone."

37
00:01:44.568 --> 00:01:47.190
I was like, "Oh my gosh, how much has changed?"

38
00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.850
So much is changing.

39
00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:52.050
Now, how did you get that resource?

40
00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.420
How did you get that memo that there was nothing

41
00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.230
you couldn't do, or did you feel like that as a kid?

42
00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:00.050
My mom and I have talked about this question a lot,

43
00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:03.700
because when I was growing up in Little Rock, in Arkansas,

44
00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:08.000
in the '80s and '90s, I had my mom as a role model.

45
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.000
I also had most of my teachers and my principals,

46
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:16.930
who were women, my pediatrician was a woman.

47
00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:21.330
The mayor of Little Rock when I was a kid was a woman.

48
00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:26.057
And so I just had such a profoundly different experience

49
00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:30.900
than my mom had had when she was growing up

50
00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:37.130
in suburban Chicago where most of her teachers were men,

51
00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:39.700
the principals were all men,

52
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.760
the people that she saw in elected office were men,

53
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:46.430
any doctor or dentist that she interacted with was a man.

54
00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:48.500
She just had such a different experience

55
00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.070
than I did a generation later.

56
00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.490
And so I think, yes, it was the power of my mother's example

57
00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.690
and my amazing grandmother's examples,

58
00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:59.140
and it also was just the world that I was growing up in.

59
00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:01.660
And so I do think we have to continue

60
00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.090
to have affirmative examples

61
00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:08.520
of strong, amazing, persistent women

62
00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.410
who have done remarkable things in many different fields,

63
00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:14.760
because it matters to me

64
00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.060
as the mom of a daughter and two sons,

65
00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:19.990
that all of them look up to women.

66
00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:23.380
I think it's really exciting for me that I see,

67
00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:26.340
in my son, who's five,

68
00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.690
if you ask him what an athlete looks like,

69
00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:30.270
he talks about Megan Rapinoe.

70
00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.393
That's what he sees as an athlete.

71
00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.060
Partly 'cause he loves her pink hair,

72
00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:36.670
and admittedly also 'cause he loves soccer,

73
00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:38.240
and our American women are a little better

74
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.505
than our American men at the moment on the soccer field.

75
00:03:42.505 --> 00:03:44.170
And so I see that it matters,

76
00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:45.740
'cause I see it in my own kids.

77
00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:46.573 line:15%
Yep.

78
00:03:46.573 --> 00:03:49.013 line:15%
(upbeat music)

